\p{\strong{8:22 AM}}
\hr
\p{A good example explanation of today's quote per ChatGPT.}
\example{ Imagine you spend years preparing for your dream job but don't get the offer.
The untrained response is:

\quotes{
"This shouldn't have happened. Everything is ruined."
}{Non-stoic}

The Stoic response is:
\quotes{
"I wanted this job, and I worked hard for it. But getting it was never completely in my control. I'll accept this outcome without bitterness and continue acting well."
}{Stoic}
Notice that the Stoic still preferred getting the job. Acceptance comes \strong{after} reality has spoken. Importantly, this is \strong{never} used as a reason to avoid working hard for the job in the first place.
}
\p{Finished my daily reading of [[hofstadter1999godel]]. Brief summary:}
\ul{
  \li{We had our first dialogue between the tortoise and Achilles.}
  \li{Zeno and his paradox were presented.}
  \li{I had a little fun playing around in the \code{MIU} system, and the interesting question of playing within vs. outside a system arose.}
  \li{He argued a distinction between humands and computers was that computers were able to effectively \em{turn off} their "thinking".}
  \example{Say, for example, in the \code{MIU} system we give the computer the list of transformations and tell it to continue until it shows that \code{U} is in the grammar. The computer will infinitely apply rules with no thought, where a human will quickly be able to tell this is impossible and will stop trying to do so.}
}
